WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.832 --> 00:00:10.294 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the North America capitals, episode two of five. First, I clear the
chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.947 --> 00:00:19.179 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Seven capitals run down the narrow land between Mexico and Colombia, with
Belize on its Caribbean shore.

3
00:00:19.847 --> 00:00:22.099 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Belmopan into view.

4
00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:33.698 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Belmopan stands inland in the Cayo district of northern Belize, where the
foothills of the Maya Mountains rise behind it.

5
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:50.658 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Built inland after Hurricane Hattie wrecked Belize City in 1961, it is one of
the smallest capitals in the world: only a few tens of thousands of people
live there, and its National Assembly building was designed to look like a
Maya temple.

6
00:00:51.311 --> 00:00:57.871 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Belmopan as the hurricane-proof capital, rebuilt inland and shaped
like a Maya temple.

7
00:00:58.545 --> 00:01:01.146 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Guatemala City into view.

8
00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:13.316 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Guatemala City lies in a highland valley at about 1,500 metres, on the chain
of volcanoes that runs through Guatemala.

9
00:01:13.981 --> 00:01:22.642 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The capital moved here after earthquakes destroyed Antigua Guatemala in 1773,
and it is the largest city in Central America.

10
00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:32.169 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Guatemala City as the capital that replaced a ruined Antigua, and the
largest city in Central America.

11
00:01:32.842 --> 00:01:36.638 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tegucigalpa into view.

12
00:01:38.816 --> 00:01:46.920 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tegucigalpa lies in a valley of the Honduran highlands, on the Choluteca
river, in the centre of the country.

13
00:01:47.572 --> 00:02:00.668 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
A name from Nahuatl whose meaning is disputed; it shares its role as capital
with Comayagüela, across the river, and its airport, Toncontín, is known as
one of the hardest in the world to land at.

14
00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:10.965 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Tegucigalpa as the two-city capital, with Comayagüela across the
river and the world's hardest landing at Toncontín.

15
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:14.527 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring San Salvador into view.

16
00:02:23.123 --> 00:02:33.015 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
San Salvador lies in the Valley of the Hammocks, below the San Salvador
volcano, on the Pacific side of El Salvador.

17
00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:44.421 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Rebuilt many times after earthquakes, it lost its archbishop Óscar Romero, who
was shot while saying Mass in 1980 and was made a saint in 2018.

18
00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:53.313 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember San Salvador as the city in the Valley of the Hammocks, rebuilt after
earthquakes and the home of Saint Óscar Romero.

19
00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:55.858 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Managua into view.

20
00:02:59.525 --> 00:03:05.620 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Managua lies on the southern shore of Lake Managua, in the lowlands of central
Nicaragua.

21
00:03:06.272 --> 00:03:17.472 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Made the capital in 1852 as a compromise between the rival cities of León and
Granada; an earthquake in 1972 destroyed its centre.

22
00:03:18.122 --> 00:03:25.204 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Managua as the compromise capital, chosen to settle the old rivalry
between León and Granada.

23
00:03:25.903 --> 00:03:28.794 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring San José into view.

24
00:03:32.456 --> 00:03:42.557 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
San José lies in the central valley of Costa Rica, between the mountains and
the Pacific, at about 1,170 metres above the sea.

25
00:03:43.222 --> 00:04:00.416 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The capital since 1823, in 1884 it was among the first cities in the world
with electric street lighting, and its National Theatre, of 1897, was paid for
by a tax on coffee exports.

26
00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:10.401 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember San José as the coffee-tax theatre of 1897, and one of the first
cities on earth lit by electricity.

27
00:04:11.071 --> 00:04:14.740 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Panama City into view.

28
00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:24.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Panama City stands on the Pacific side of the isthmus, where the canal cuts
through the narrow land between the two oceans.

29
00:04:25.619 --> 00:04:45.059 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Founded in 1519, the first European city on the Pacific coast of the Americas;
the pirate Henry Morgan sacked the old city in 1671, and it was rebuilt a few
kilometres away. It is often said to be the only capital with a rainforest
inside its limits.

30
00:04:45.721 --> 00:04:54.290 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Panama City as the first Pacific city of the Americas, rebuilt after
Henry Morgan's sack, with rainforest inside its limits.

31
00:04:58.794 --> 00:05:11.287 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Belmopan and Guatemala City sit in the north of the isthmus, Tegucigalpa, San
Salvador and Managua in the middle, and San José and Panama City at the narrow
southern end.

32
00:05:11.946 --> 00:05:30.558 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the hurricane-proof capital shaped like a Maya temple, the capital
that replaced a ruined Antigua, the two-city capital with Comayagüela, the
Valley of the Hammocks, the compromise between León and Granada, the
coffee-tax theatre and the first Pacific city of the Americas.
